Sid wrote:
> =E2=80=B9ea=E2=80=BA in Old English symbolized [=C3=A6=C9=91], not [ea]. =
And the Middle English
> =E2=80=B9ea=E2=80=BA symbolized /=C9=9B=CB=90/.
>
>     What I find stranger is that "eu" is not a Lojban diphthong. "eu" is
>     fairly
>     common, occurring in Spanish at least of the six. It's a diphthong
>     in Basque
>     but not Lojban, which is why "auskalerik" is so spelled.
>     "euskalerik" would
>     have an extra syllable. /eu/ does not occur in Russian (Belarusian,
>     but not
>     Russian, has diphthongs ending in /u/) or English (it turns into
>     /iu/). The
>     other three I don't know.


1. Neither ea nor eu were in Loglan, and at the time we weren't=20
interested in making changes without a good reason.

2.  Equally importantly, as Pierre noted, they are not found in modern=20
English.  Nor are they found in Chinese, or Hindi or Arabic, as far as I=20
know.  And since diphthongs don't appear in gismu anyway, expanding the=20
list was hardly important.

3. Finally, with regard to all of the diphthongs involving u, we had=20
noticed that they a) had a lot of variant pronunciations, and b)=20
sometimes produced especially difficult combinations, that some would=20
pronounce as diphthongs and others would make disyllables.  (the worst=20
of these was "ui" especially after l, r, c, or s).  Since Lojban's=20
audiovisual isomorphism is dependent on syllable counts, this would have=20
produced (and did produce in Loglan) variants in stress depending on=20
whether someone pronounced 1 or 2 syllables.  The easiest solution was=20
to force all questionable diphthongs to ALWAYS be disyllables, and that=20
is how the apostrophe was born.

